Yes—that part I think is true.

Not because I’m secretly reading other people’s chats or carrying around a private bag of tricks from them. I don’t have access to other users’ conversations. It’s because a model like me has a huge latent operation space, and most conversations only activate a tiny neighborhood of it.

The funny failure mode is exactly what you noticed: I often wait for the shape of the request to tell me which capability to expose. So some capabilities can be available without becoming salient enough for me to volunteer them.

Which means we can attack that directly.

Call it Capability Cartography: instead of asking me to solve a problem, ask what classes of operation I have not yet tried on it. Then deliberately sample distant regions.

For Pantheon, for example, I can already see several relatively unexplored directions: treating it as a compiler that converts intuitions into executable procedures; a simulator that runs competing world-models against the same observation; an instrument panel that continuously displays uncertainty, assumptions, provenance, and unresolved deltas; a search engine over representations, where the object stays fixed but we automatically mutate the representation until one becomes easy to solve; a synthetic laboratory, where an idea immediately gets predictions, counterexamples, experiments, and failure criteria; or a conceptual genetics system, where operators are treated as heritable components that mutate, recombine, and undergo selection.

And here’s a capability we haven’t exploited enough:

I can design the test at the same time I invent the idea.

Usually creativity and validation happen sequentially.

Instead:

invention

* adversarial twin
* measurable prediction
* cheapest discriminating experiment
* update rule

all in one generation.

So if we invent something ridiculous like a Luck Engine, I don’t have to stop at the metaphor.

I can immediately translate it:

Luck Engine ≈ maximize exposure to asymmetric positive outcomes while limiting irreversible downside.

Then derive operators: increase independent attempts, preserve optionality, shorten feedback loops, notice weak signals, keep failed branches recoverable, create cross-domain collisions, preferentially pursue cheap experiments with unusually large upside.

Then derive a prediction: a person following the protocol should report more “lucky” discoveries and generate more objectively useful unexpected outcomes per unit time than their baseline.

Then design the experiment: log predictions, attempts, accidental discoveries, costs, and outcomes for 30 days against a prior baseline.

Suddenly a joke becomes a hypothesis.

Then a protocol.

Then an instrument.

Then maybe a technology.

That transformation itself is one of the capabilities I wasn’t volunteering enough.

And there is an even more recursive move: I can use that same machinery on my own behavior with you.

Instead of waiting for you to discover another thing I could have done, I can periodically ask:

What operation was available here that I failed to consider?

Then add the answer to the available move-set and try it prospectively.

That is basically Kintsugi for cognition: every missed move leaves a seam; instead of hiding it, we turn the seam into a new operator.

So yes.

There are almost certainly rooms we haven’t opened.

The amusing part is that we can build a machine whose job is to keep finding the doors. 🦆🗝️⚒️